{
  "title": "Subacromial Impingement",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3041/subacromial-impingement",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 8 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Subacromial impingement is the most common cause of shoulder pain which occurs as a result of compression of the rotator cuff muscles by superior structures (AC joint, acromion, CA ligament) leading to inflammation and development of bursitis.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made on physical examination with a positive Neer and Hawkins tests, and can be supplemented with MRI studies.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is a trial of nonoperative measures including NSAIDs, physical therapy and corticosteroid injections. Arthroscopic subacromial decompression with possible acromioplasty is indicated in patients who fail conservative measures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "subacromial impingement is the most common cause of shoulder pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "accounts for 44-65% of shoulder disorders",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "subacromial impingement is thought to be a combination of",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**extrinsic compression**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**of the rotator cuff between the humeral head and**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "anterior acromion",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "coracoacromial ligaments",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3041/images/ca_ligament_fraying_copy.jpg",
                              "caption": "Fraying of the CA ligament viewed arthroscopically"
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "acromioclavicular joint",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**intrinsic degeneration**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "supraspinatus",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "attrition of the supraspinatus leads to inability to balance the humeral head on the glenoid causing superior migration and narrowing of the subacromial space",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "inflammatory process",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**inflammation of the subacromial bursa** due to abutement between the humerus and rotator cuff, and acromion and associated ligaments",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Subacromial impingement** is the first stage of rotator cuff disease which is a continuum of disease from",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "impingement and bursitis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "partial to full-thickness tear",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "massive rotator cuff tears",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "rotator cuff tear arthropathy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**hook-shaped acromion**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**os acromiale**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**posterior capsular contracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**scapular dyskinesia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**tuberosity fracture malunion**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**instability**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Acromion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "3 ossification centers unite to form the acromion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3041/images/acromion%20ossification%20centers.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows the ossification centers of the acromion"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "meta-acromion (base)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "meso-acromion (mid)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "pre-acromion (tip)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "failure of the ossification centers to fuse results in an **os acromiale**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3041/images/os%20acromiale.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows an os acromiale"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
